1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,900
Agents draft and send documents in seconds. The hard part is authority: did a person approve this file for this signer?

2
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:19,000
SignGate binds one document, one recipient, and one provider attempt into a chain of custody.

3
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:31,000
A brief becomes an agreement. Foxit's official MCP renders and downloads the PDF, then deletes the remote files.

4
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:41,000
Before release, the reviewer sees exact content, its full hash, recipient, legal warning, email risk, and provider mode.

5
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:49,000
Approval isn't a button. It requires a hash-bound phrase, recipient confirmation, and signing authority.

6
00:00:49,120 --> 00:01:03,000
Now the adversarial test. After approval, I change the recipient. SignGate fails closed: the binding breaks, provider calls remain at zero, and the original consent remains unspent.

7
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:17,000
On a fresh artifact, a valid request consumes its one-shot receipt before dispatch. This public demo completes the workflow without sending email or pretending that Foxit created an envelope.

8
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:29,000
Foxit is load-bearing, not a logo. Its PDF MCP handles reversible document work; eSign runs only after the human gate.

9
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:36,000
The audit chain records every outcome: blocked, uncertain, sent, or complete.

10
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:44,000
The public repository includes source, adversarial tests, and a privacy-safe record of the provider run.

11
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:58,000
In Foxit's sandbox, a consented run rendered the document, sent one eSign invitation, downloaded and re-hashed the result, then recovered after a cold restart.

12
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:11,000
The final PDF passed structural checks, showed a signature, and carried a valid detached SHA-256 signature. The validation revision rendered pixel-identically.

13
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:19,000
Sandbox proof, not legal validity. AI proposes. Human consent authorizes.
